{"id":"~Dongjie_Zhu2","name":"Dongjie Zhu","counts":{"accepted_papers":2,"first_position_papers":0,"co_first_author_papers":null,"corresponding_author_papers":null,"Poster only":2},"papers":[{"id":"h4x4aofBgS","title":"UniLab: A Heterogeneous Architecture for Robot RL Beyond GPU-Dominant Paradigms","abstract":"Simulation-based RL for contemporary robot control is increasingly organized around GPU-resident simulation: physics, rollout collection, and learning are placed on a single GPU-centric execution path. This paradigm has greatly improved training speed, but it has also encouraged a default assumption that efficient training requires physics to reside on the GPU. We revisit this assumption. Our view is that, in simulation-dominated robot control, the essential question is not which processor runs physics, but whether simulation throughput, policy learning, and runtime synchronization form an efficient end-to-end loop. We present UniLab, a heterogeneous CPU-simulation / GPU-learning architecture that decouples CPU-parallel simulation from GPU policy updates through a unified runtime for data movement, buffering, and synchronization. UniLab is implemented as a complete and extensible training system using MuJoCoUni and MotrixSim CPU-batched physics backends, supporting PPO, FastSAC, FlashSAC, and APPO. On representative simulation-based robot control tasks, UniLab improves end-to-end training efficiency by 3--10$\\times$ under the same hardware configuration, while reducing dependence on the NVIDIA CUDA-based software stack and supporting cross-platform execution on the Apple macOS platform and the AMD ROCm and Intel XPU accelerator backends. These results show that GPU simulation is an effective path to efficient training, but not a necessary one, broadening the practical system choices available for robot RL training.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["强化学习与奖励","世界模型与仿真"],"title_topics":["强化学习与奖励"],"authors":["~Yufei_Jia1","~Zhanxiang_Cao1","~Mingrui_Yu1","~Heng_Zhang40","~Shenyu_Chen1","~Dixuan_Jiang1","~Meng_Li47","~Xiaofan_Li8","~Yiyang_Liu9","~junzhe_wu3","~Zheng_Li56","~xilin_fang1","~Ting-Yu_Tsui1","~Shengcheng_Fu1","~Haoyang_Li24","~Anqi_Wang6","~Zifan_Wang7","~Dongjie_Zhu2","~Chenyu_Cao2","~Zhenbiao_Huang1","~Ziang_Zheng1","~Jie_Lu18","~Xin_Ma24","~魏正阳1","~Xiang_Zhao8","~Tianyue_Zhan1","~He_Ye4","~Yuxiang_Chen10","~Yizhou_Jiang3","~Yue_Li52","~Haizhou_Ge1","~Yuhang_Dong4","~Fan_Jia7","~Ziheng_Zhang5","~Meng_Zhang12","~Xiwa_Deng1","~Zhixing_Chen3","~Hanyang_Shao3","~Chenxin_Dong1","~Yixuan_Li7","~Yizhi_Chen3","~Bokui_Chen1","~Kaifeng_Zhang1","~Hanqing_Cui1","~Yusen_Qin1","~Ruqi_Huang1","~Lei_Han11","~Tiancai_Wang1","~Xiang_Li45","~Yue_Gao8","~Guyue_Zhou2"],"institutions":["AMD","Beijing Institute of Technology","D-Robotics","DISCOVER Robotics","Dexmal","Galbot","Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou)","Motphys","Nanjing University","National University of Singapore","Shanghai Innovation Institute","Shanghai Jiao Tong University","Sharpa","Southern University of Science and Technology","Tianjin University","Tsinghua University","Zhejiang University"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.30313","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=h4x4aofBgS","project":["https://unilabsim.github.io","https://unilabsim.github.io/paper"],"code":["https://github.com/unilabsim/UniLab","https://github.com/Motphys/UniLab"],"poster_date":"2026-11-11","poster_time":"10:30 AM–12:00 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":70,"hardware":[{"model":"Sharpa 灵巧手 / Wave","env":["simulation"],"reviewed":true}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"h4x4aofBgS","position":18,"first":false,"roles":[]}},{"id":"317SZV2vuS","title":"PROOF: Perceptive Robustness via Out-of-Distribution Fallback for Legged Locomotion","abstract":"Vision-based perceptive locomotion has enabled legged robots to achieve agile and terrain-aware locomotion across complex environments. However, the reliability of such systems heavily depends on visual observations, which are inherently susceptible to occlusion, reflective surfaces, and illumination variations, often resulting in catastrophic locomotion failures under degraded perception conditions. In this paper, we present PROOF (Perceptive Robustness via Out-of-Distribution Fallback for Legged Locomotion), a perceptive locomotion framework that enables robust locomotion under severe visual disturbances. Our method adopts a two-stage teacher–student learning paradigm that explicitly models perception reliability: the teacher learns both perception-driven and proprioceptive fallback behaviors, while the student predicts exteroceptive features with uncertainty and suppresses them under out-of-distribution inputs. We evaluate our method on a Unitree Go2 quadruped equipped with a RealSense D435 depth camera in real-world settings. Under normal visual conditions, our method enables the robot to successfully perform high-dynamic parkour maneuvers, including traversing large gaps, elevated platforms, and narrow beam terrains. Under severe visual degradation, such as heavy occlusion and strong illumination changes the robot continues to maintain stable and robust locomotion performance.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["移动、全身与人形","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"title_topics":["移动、全身与人形","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"authors":["~Siyuan_Wang23","~Dongjie_Zhu2","~Jinxuan_Lu2","~Wenjun_Xu2","~Jiankun_WANG2"],"institutions":[],"pdf":"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=317SZV2vuS","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=317SZV2vuS","project":[],"code":[],"poster_date":"2026-11-11","poster_time":"10:30 AM–12:00 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":169,"hardware":[],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"317SZV2vuS","position":2,"first":false,"roles":[]}}],"topics":{"强化学习与奖励":1,"世界模型与仿真":1,"移动、全身与人形":1,"安全、泛化与鲁棒性":1},"role_counts":{},"role_papers_reviewed":0,"profile":null,"portfolio":null,"needs_identifier_r